sections.content:
To really appreciate why Template Method exists, consider what happens without it. You write three report classes, each with its own `generate()` method:

```js
class CsvReport {
  generate(data) {
    const raw = data;  // forgot to log, will add later
    const csv = this._toCSV(raw);
    fs.writeFileSync('report.csv', csv);
  }
}

class HtmlReport {
  generate(data) {
    const raw = fetchFromApi(data);  // different fetching strategy!
    const html = this._toHTML(raw);
    const validated = this._validate(html);  // added for HTML only
    fs.writeFileSync('report.html', validated);
  }
}

class PdfReport {
  generate(data) {
    const raw = data;
    const pdf = this._toPDF(raw);
    // step 3 was forgotten entirely
    writeToS3('report.pdf', pdf);
  }
}
```

Three classes, three different shapes of `generate()`. CsvReport skips validation. PdfReport uses S3 but the others use the filesystem. HtmlReport fetches from an API but CsvReport takes raw data. Now imagine:
- A new requirement: all reports must log their generation time.
- A security audit: all reports must call `sanitize()` before writing.
- A new report type: ExcelReport.

Every change requires updating multiple places, with high risk of each class diverging further. The core issue is that the **orchestration logic is not in one place**. Template Method collapses it to one:

```js
class ReportGenerator {
  generate(data) {
    console.time('report');
    const raw = this.fetchData(data);
    const formatted = this.formatData(raw);
    const safe = sanitize(formatted);  // one change, affects all
    this.beforeWrite();
    const result = this.writeOutput(safe);
    console.timeEnd('report');
    return result;
  }
}
```

Adding logging and sanitisation is now a single-line change in one method. All report types benefit automatically.

