// ── Template Method vs Strategy: side-by-side ────────────────────────

// TEMPLATE METHOD — skeleton fixed in base class via inheritance
class DataProcessor {
  process(input) {
    const validated  = this.validate(input);   // abstract
    const normalized = this.normalize(validated); // abstract
    this.onComplete(normalized);               // hook
    return normalized;
  }
  validate(input)      { throw new Error('validate() required'); }
  normalize(data)      { throw new Error('normalize() required'); }
  onComplete(result)   { /* optional hook */ }
}

class UpperCaseProcessor extends DataProcessor {
  validate(s) {
    if (typeof s !== 'string') throw new TypeError('Expected string');
    return s.trim();
  }
  normalize(s)   { return s.toUpperCase(); }
  onComplete(r)  { console.log('[TM] Processed:', r); }
}

console.log(new UpperCaseProcessor().process('  hello  '));
// [TM] Processed: HELLO
// → 'HELLO'

// STRATEGY — skeleton in Context; algorithm injected from outside
class ProcessorContext {
  constructor(strategy) {
    this._strategy = strategy;
  }
  setStrategy(strategy) {
    this._strategy = strategy;  // swap at runtime!
  }
  process(input) {
    const validated  = this._strategy.validate(input);
    const normalized = this._strategy.normalize(validated);
    return normalized;
  }
}

const upperCaseStrategy = {
  validate:  (s) => { if (typeof s !== 'string') throw new TypeError(); return s.trim(); },
  normalize: (s) => s.toUpperCase(),
};

const lowerCaseStrategy = {
  validate:  (s) => { if (typeof s !== 'string') throw new TypeError(); return s.trim(); },
  normalize: (s) => s.toLowerCase(),
};

const ctx = new ProcessorContext(upperCaseStrategy);
console.log(ctx.process('  hello  '));  // 'HELLO'

ctx.setStrategy(lowerCaseStrategy);    // runtime swap — impossible with TM
console.log(ctx.process('  WORLD  ')); // 'world'

// Summary:
// Template Method: simpler, tighter coupling, skeleton is the class hierarchy
// Strategy: flexible, loose coupling, skeleton is the context

sections.content:
JavaScript's lack of `abstract` and `final` keywords makes Template Method a convention rather than a compile-time guarantee. TypeScript improves this significantly with `abstract` classes and methods.

```ts
abstract class ReportGenerator<TData, TResult> {
  // Template method — not abstract, should not be overridden
  generate(data: TData[]): TResult {
    const raw       = this.fetchData(data);
    const formatted = this.formatData(raw);
    this.beforeWrite();
    const result    = this.writeOutput(formatted);
    this.afterWrite();
    return result;
  }

  // Default step — overridable
  protected fetchData(data: TData[]): TData[] {
    return data;
  }

  // Abstract steps — TypeScript enforces override
  protected abstract formatData(raw: TData[]): string;
  protected abstract writeOutput(content: string): TResult;

  // Hooks — optional, no-op defaults
  protected beforeWrite(): void {}
  protected afterWrite(): void  {}
}

class CsvReport extends ReportGenerator<Record<string, unknown>, string> {
  protected formatData(rows: Record<string, unknown>[]): string {
    if (!rows.length) return '';
    const header = Object.keys(rows[0]).join(',');
    const body   = rows.map(r => Object.values(r).join(',')).join('\n');
    return `${header}\n${body}`;
  }

  protected writeOutput(content: string): string {
    return `FILE:report.csv\n${content}`;
  }
}
```

Key TypeScript advantages:
- `abstract class` prevents direct instantiation of the base class
- `abstract` methods create a compile-time error if a subclass omits them — no runtime surprise
- `protected` visibility enforces that steps are only called by the template method, not by external callers
- Generics (`TData`, `TResult`) let the base class be type-safe while still generic enough for multiple report types

The one remaining gap: TypeScript cannot mark a method `final` to prevent override of `generate()` itself. The convention is to document it and use `protected` access modifiers to signal intent. If you need hard enforcement, a wrapper class pattern or a factory function that freezes the skeleton method can be used.

Modern JavaScript leans heavily on functions and closures over class hierarchies. You can express the Template Method pattern functionally using **higher-order functions** — the skeleton is a function that accepts callbacks for each step:

```js
function createReportGenerator({ formatData, writeOutput, beforeWrite, afterWrite, fetchData }) {
  return {
    generate(data) {
      const raw       = fetchData ? fetchData(data) : data;
      const formatted = formatData(raw);        // required
      if (beforeWrite) beforeWrite();
      const result    = writeOutput(formatted); // required
      if (afterWrite)  afterWrite();
      return result;
    }
  };
}

const csvReport = createReportGenerator({
  formatData:  (rows) => rows.map(r => Object.values(r).join(',')).join('\n'),
  writeOutput: (content) => ({ file: 'report.csv', content }),
});

const htmlReport = createReportGenerator({
  fetchData:   (data) => data.map(r => ({ ...r, _source: 'api' })),
  formatData:  (rows) => `<table>${rows.map(r => `<tr><td>${Object.values(r).join('</td><td>')}</td></tr>`).join('')}</table>`,
  writeOutput: (content) => ({ file: 'report.html', content }),
  beforeWrite: () => console.log('Validating HTML...'),
});
```

This is Template Method without inheritance. The `createReportGenerator` factory *is* the AbstractClass — it defines the skeleton. The options object *is* the ConcreteClass — it provides step implementations. Required steps throw (or could be checked at factory time); optional hooks are guarded with `if`.

This functional style is popular in modern JS codebases because:
- No class syntax needed
- Steps are plain functions — easy to unit-test in isolation
- Configuration is explicit and visible at construction time
- No prototype chain confusion

The trade-off: you lose TypeScript's `abstract` enforcement. Required steps must be validated at factory-call time or at runtime. A TypeScript discriminated union or `Required<>` type can enforce this at the type level.
